WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.180
Two diseases both make muscles weak.

2
00:00:06.140 --> 00:00:07.600
How can a recording tell them apart?

3
00:00:08.633 --> 00:00:12.553
Return to the rule: The response ≈ count × size.

4
00:00:13.617 --> 00:00:18.057
A muscle weakens when the response falls below what the fibre needs to fire.

5
00:00:19.067 --> 00:00:24.767
In myasthenia gravis, antibodies in most patients target the muscle's acetylcholine

6
00:00:24.767 --> 00:00:25.767
receptors.

7
00:00:26.350 --> 00:00:29.090
Fewer receptors means a smaller packet size.

8
00:00:30.083 --> 00:00:35.463
An immunoglobulin fraction from patients' serum, moved into mice, gave them smaller

9
00:00:35.463 --> 00:00:37.183
miniatures and fewer receptors.

10
00:00:38.333 --> 00:00:43.133
Take the made-up junction, and suppose the first impulse releases exactly sixty

11
00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:47.353
packets. Cut the size, and that response still fires, but barely.

12
00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:53.567
But release runs down during repeated impulses, as ready vesicles get used up.

13
00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:57.567
Suppose a later impulse releases a quarter fewer packets.

14
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:00.380
The normal fibre still fires.

15
00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:04.280
The myasthenic fibre drops below threshold and fails.

16
00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:10.560
Real counts also vary from impulse to impulse, so a response near threshold can fail

17
00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:11.560
even earlier.

18
00:01:12.383 --> 00:01:14.023
This is fatigable weakness.

19
00:01:15.063 --> 00:01:16.943
Muscles work at first and weaken with use.

20
00:01:17.967 --> 00:01:23.347
Drugs that block acetylcholinesterase let each packet's acetylcholine last longer.

21
00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:26.477
It reaches more of the remaining receptors.

22
00:01:27.397 --> 00:01:30.077
That raises the size part of the way back toward normal.

23
00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:34.350
In Lambert-Eaton syndrome, the fault is in the terminal.

24
00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:39.070
Antibodies against its calcium channels cut calcium entry.

25
00:01:39.850 --> 00:01:42.110
So the count is low, while the size is normal.

26
00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:46.090
Now stimulate rapidly, 20 Hz.

27
00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:48.630
What happens to the count during the train?

28
00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:59.350
It rises. Calcium left over from one impulse adds to the next.

29
00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:05.430
Because release rises steeply with calcium, a little extra calcium brings a large

30
00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:06.430
rise in the count.

31
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:11.200
Slow stimulation can instead give a decrement, a shrinking response.

32
00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.100
Calcium clears between impulses, and run-down wins.

33
00:02:16.183 --> 00:02:18.663
So the two diseases leave opposite fingerprints.

34
00:02:19.717 --> 00:02:23.017
A smaller size with a normal count points to the size side.

35
00:02:23.797 --> 00:02:25.897
That means receptors, or vesicle filling.

36
00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:30.870
A lower count with a normal size points to release from the terminal.

37
00:02:31.883 --> 00:02:36.203
One caution. Poorly filled vesicles also shrink the size.

38
00:02:36.863 --> 00:02:39.043
So pair the recording with a receptor count.

39
00:02:40.067 --> 00:02:42.647
Finally, a synapse with no packets at all.

40
00:02:43.427 --> 00:02:46.567
At an electrical synapse, gap junctions join the two cells.

41
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:50.180
Current passes directly, with no vesicles.

42
00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:54.220
So there is no quantal count or size, and no release failures.

43
00:02:55.283 --> 00:02:57.283
The coupled response still has a size.

44
00:02:58.123 --> 00:02:59.983
It can be too small to fire the next cell.

45
00:03:00.933 --> 00:03:03.953
Most are very fast, and pass current both ways.

46
00:03:03.953 --> 00:03:07.693
Some rectify, passing current more easily one way.

47
00:03:08.633 --> 00:03:12.473
At this junction, response ≈ count × size.

48
00:03:13.453 --> 00:03:14.733
Ask which one moved, or both.
